1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,183
Kiran and Salim chacha must move an
old auto rickshaw that will not start.

2
00:00:05,483 --> 00:00:06,488
Kiran pushes.

3
00:00:06,488 --> 00:00:07,906
Salim pulls a rope.

4
00:00:07,906 --> 00:00:10,389
But the auto swerves toward the wall.

5
00:00:10,939 --> 00:00:11,839
Why?

6
00:00:11,412 --> 00:00:14,931
They pushed hard, but forgot
which way each force points.

7
00:00:15,581 --> 00:00:20,508
Meet Salim Auto Works, a small
repair workshop in Peenya, Bengaluru.

8
00:00:20,858 --> 00:00:24,059
Salim chacha has fixed
autos for thirty years.

9
00:00:24,059 --> 00:00:27,239
His nephew Kiran studies
mechanical engineering.

10
00:00:27,589 --> 00:00:32,296
With Priya, Raju and Farida, they will
turn it into an electric auto called

11
00:00:32,296 --> 00:00:33,196
Bijli.

12
00:00:33,539 --> 00:00:35,791
So what is engineering mechanics?

13
00:00:36,091 --> 00:00:37,695
In his textbook, R.

14
00:00:37,695 --> 00:00:38,595
C.

15
00:00:38,122 --> 00:00:42,630
Hibbeler says mechanics is the science
of the rest or motion of bodies

16
00:00:42,630 --> 00:00:44,129
acted on by forces.

17
00:00:44,479 --> 00:00:47,165
Bodies at rest are studied in statics.

18
00:00:47,165 --> 00:00:50,007
Bodies in motion are studied in dynamics.

19
00:00:50,357 --> 00:00:54,469
In simple words: find every force,
and you can predict what a thing will

20
00:00:54,469 --> 00:00:55,369
do.

21
00:00:55,523 --> 00:00:56,963
So what is a force?

22
00:00:56,963 --> 00:00:59,008
A force is a push or a pull.

23
00:00:59,308 --> 00:01:01,694
It has a size, measured in newtons.

24
00:01:01,694 --> 00:01:05,158
One newton is about the weight
of a hundred gram apple.

25
00:01:05,508 --> 00:01:09,642
It has a direction, and a point where
it acts, on a line called its line

26
00:01:09,642 --> 00:01:10,542
of action.

27
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:14,151
Size and direction together
make a force a vector.

28
00:01:14,151 --> 00:01:15,976
We draw it as an arrow.

29
00:01:16,626 --> 00:01:19,297
Now let's replay the push, like engineers.

30
00:01:19,647 --> 00:01:22,471
Kiran pushes forward
with two hundred newtons.

31
00:01:22,821 --> 00:01:27,083
Salim pulls with three hundred newtons,
but his rope points thirty degrees

32
00:01:27,083 --> 00:01:28,044
to the side.

33
00:01:28,394 --> 00:01:30,111
Put the arrows tip to tail.

34
00:01:30,111 --> 00:01:34,203
The total, the resultant, is about
four hundred and eighty four newtons,

35
00:01:34,203 --> 00:01:35,859
eighteen degrees off course.

36
00:01:36,209 --> 00:01:38,604
So the auto drifts toward the wall.

37
00:01:38,604 --> 00:01:42,364
One hundred and fifty newtons
of Salim's pull goes sideways.

38
00:01:43,014 --> 00:01:45,438
Now Salim stands in
front and pulls straight.

39
00:01:45,788 --> 00:01:49,014
Both forces lie on one
line, so they simply add.

40
00:01:49,014 --> 00:01:51,036
Two hundred plus three hundred.

41
00:01:51,386 --> 00:01:55,022
Five hundred newtons, all of
it forward, on a straight path.

42
00:01:55,672 --> 00:01:57,809
A force is not just how hard.

43
00:01:57,809 --> 00:01:59,422
It is also which way.

44
00:02:00,122 --> 00:02:03,681
A group of forces acting together
is called a force system.

45
00:02:04,031 --> 00:02:04,931
Collinear.

46
00:02:04,776 --> 00:02:08,234
All the forces lie on one
line, like the straight pull.

47
00:02:08,534 --> 00:02:09,434
Concurrent.

48
00:02:09,353 --> 00:02:13,634
The lines of action meet at one point,
like the angled rope and Kiran's

49
00:02:13,634 --> 00:02:14,534
push.

50
00:02:14,467 --> 00:02:15,367
Parallel.

51
00:02:15,219 --> 00:02:19,747
The lines never meet, like the ground
pushing up on all three wheels.

52
00:02:20,047 --> 00:02:20,947
General.

53
00:02:20,781 --> 00:02:24,978
Neither meeting nor parallel,
like wind and weight on a roof.

54
00:02:24,978 --> 00:02:28,379
Forces off one flat plane
are called non coplanar.

55
00:02:29,029 --> 00:02:30,996
Where do we see this at work today?

56
00:02:31,346 --> 00:02:32,246
Collinear.

57
00:02:32,162 --> 00:02:35,987
A railway engine pulls its
coaches along one straight line.

58
00:02:36,337 --> 00:02:37,237
Concurrent.

59
00:02:37,171 --> 00:02:40,684
A crane lifts a load on
slings that meet at one hook.

60
00:02:41,034 --> 00:02:41,934
Parallel.

61
00:02:41,730 --> 00:02:45,684
A bridge deck rests on its pillars,
each one pushing straight up.

62
00:02:46,034 --> 00:02:46,934
General.

63
00:02:46,711 --> 00:02:50,778
Wind and weight push on a tall
signboard from different directions.

64
00:02:51,428 --> 00:02:53,861
But is this way of thinking always enough?

65
00:02:54,211 --> 00:02:55,368
The good side.

66
00:02:55,368 --> 00:02:59,976
Simple arrows give quick, reliable
answers, from bicycles to bridges.

67
00:03:00,326 --> 00:03:01,483
The hard side.

68
00:03:01,483 --> 00:03:05,957
Real parts bend and twist, and a
forgotten force can break things.

69
00:03:06,307 --> 00:03:10,876
In nineteen forty, the Tacoma Narrows
Bridge in the United States twisted

70
00:03:10,876 --> 00:03:14,151
apart in the wind, four
months after it opened.

71
00:03:14,501 --> 00:03:16,271
So Salim makes a rule.

72
00:03:16,271 --> 00:03:20,067
Before anyone lifts or tows,
Kiran draws every force.

73
00:03:20,417 --> 00:03:21,317
The lesson?

74
00:03:21,271 --> 00:03:24,372
Every force counts, even
the ones you cannot see.

75
00:03:25,022 --> 00:03:25,922
Quick check.

76
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:28,242
Kiran pushes with two hundred newtons.

77
00:03:28,242 --> 00:03:32,240
Salim pulls the same way, on the
same line, with three hundred.

78
00:03:32,240 --> 00:03:33,859
What is the total force?

79
00:03:35,759 --> 00:03:37,179
Five hundred newtons!

80
00:03:37,179 --> 00:03:39,990
Same line, same way, so they simply add.

81
00:03:40,590 --> 00:03:41,603
So, remember.

82
00:03:41,903 --> 00:03:46,074
Mechanics studies the forces on
bodies, at rest or in motion.

83
00:03:46,424 --> 00:03:50,051
A force has a size, a direction
and a point where it acts.

84
00:03:50,051 --> 00:03:52,363
It is a vector, measured in newtons.

85
00:03:52,713 --> 00:03:57,324
And forces act in systems: collinear,
concurrent, parallel or general.

86
00:03:57,924 --> 00:04:01,519
Next time, we'll find the
resultant of concurrent forces.
